import type { SVGProps } from "react";

/**
 * Isometric people/team illustration, provided by client as
 * about_us_isometric_people_outline.svg. Rendered inline as a component
 * (not <img>/next/image) so its color is CSS-driven and it paints
 * instantly with no network request.
 *
 * GEOMETRY -- pre-merged vector fill, not a live stroke + filter
 * (2026-08-09): the source is a flat-color icon flattened to
 * outline-only -- every seam between two adjacent regions is
 * independently traced by both regions, leaving a genuine sub-pixel
 * GAP between two parallel hairlines. Two earlier fixes were tried
 * and failed:
 *   1. Widening `stroke-width` -- fat enough to touch at ONE render
 *      size, reappeared at others.
 *   2. `feMorphology` dilate+erode filter -- rasterizes relative to
 *      the element's ACTUAL ON-SCREEN PIXEL SIZE, so a radius tuned
 *      for one CSS width was wrong at another (client: "when zoomed
 *      ... creates lots of inconsistencies").
 * Both are raster techniques papering over a vector problem. Fixed
 * instead by closing the gap ONCE in true vector space -- zero
 * dependency on render size, zoom, or DPI, same as any normal
 * hand-authored vector icon.
 *
 * Offline pipeline (not run at build or runtime -- see
 * assets/about_us_isometric_people_outline.svg for the untouched
 * original):
 *   1. Parsed all 118 source `<path>` outlines into point lists.
 *   2. `ClipperLib.ClipperOffset` (round joins) dilated every shape
 *      by 0.7 source units into a `PolyTree`, so shapes a hairline
 *      gap apart start to touch -- but tracked AS A TREE, not
 *      flattened, so which shapes actually merged is still known.
 *   3. Walked the tree into 13 independent CLUSTERS: an outer
 *      contour + its direct hole children (real ring/hole geometry)
 *      stay together; a non-hole grandchild (an island inside a
 *      hole) starts a new cluster. Each cluster = one real visual
 *      element (the crate+platform as a whole; each of the 5 people
 *      as a whole; a handful of small standalone details).
 *   4. Eroded each cluster back down by the same 0.7 INDEPENDENTLY
 *      -- classic morphological closing (dilate then erode), done
 *      per-cluster rather than globally.
 *   5. Douglas-Peucker simplified each loop (0.15-unit tolerance).
 *   6. Emitted ONE `<path fill-rule="evenodd">` per cluster, all
 *      painted independently in the same `<g>`.
 *
 * WHY PER-CLUSTER, NOT ONE MERGED PATH (bug found by client
 * 2026-08-09: "behind [the 3rd person's] head, the logo line is
 * getting disappeared"): an earlier version merged ALL 118 source
 * shapes into a single fill-rule="evenodd" path. The diagonal
 * railing passes behind that head in 2D -- two UNRELATED shapes that
 * just happen to overlap, not a real seam pair. `evenodd` doesn't
 * know the difference: it XORs any overlap, so the line/head
 * crossing was treated as a hole and vanished. Switching the whole
 * thing to fill-rule="nonzero" was tried next and is WORSE -- with
 * every loop forced to one winding direction (needed so nonzero
 * doesn't itself cancel on reversed-winding overlaps), everything
 * overlapping anything else just fills solid, and this illustration
 * has real nested/hole geometry that needs the alternating logic --
 * the whole crate + all 5 people turned into solid white blobs.
 * There is no single global fill-rule that's correct for both
 * "seam pairs that must close" and "unrelated shapes that cross in
 * 2D and must stay independent" -- they need OPPOSITE handling. The
 * cluster split above is what actually distinguishes them: within a
 * cluster, evenodd is safe (any nesting there is real hole/ring
 * geometry); across clusters, separate `<path>` elements just paint
 * as independent ink with no fill-rule interaction at all -- exactly
 * how the original separate `<path>` strokes behaved before any of
 * this. Verified: the railing now runs unbroken behind every head
 * it crosses, at native size, hero size, and 200% browser zoom.
 *
 * `currentColor` + `text-foreground` keeps this on the same
 * typography color token as the rest of this column (client
 * instruction, 2026-08-09).
 *
 * If the SOURCE SVG is ever replaced, this file needs regenerating
 * from the new source via the pipeline above -- this path data is
 * derived, not hand-authored.
 *
 * PERSON 3 (middle figure) / RAIL PROXIMITY -- two fixes tried and BOTH
 * REVERTED (2026-08-09), current file has neither: the diagonal rail
 * (part of cluster index 1, the crate+platform) passes close above this
 * figure's hairline, a small ~27-unit gap, not an actual overlap, but
 * tight enough at display size to read as crowding near the head.
 *   1. `transform="translate(0, -18)"` on the person's own path (cluster
 *      index 2) -- wrong direction: the rail sits at SMALLER y than the
 *      head, so lifting the person moved the head UP INTO the rail
 *      instead of away from it, visibly crossing the forehead.
 *   2. A locally-tapered upward bump on the rail itself, restricted by
 *      x/y bounding box to just the stretch over this head -- the bounds
 *      weren't tight enough and also caught the ramp/transition curve
 *      connecting the crate to the platform (same cluster, same
 *      x-range), visibly kinking that curve (client: "entire design of
 *      the logo has been changed, it harmed the logo structure").
 * Reverted both rather than risk a third attempt distorting the crate
 * geometry again. Current state: the small ~27-unit gap is back, same as
 * right after the fill-rule fix above, with the crate/platform completely
 * unmodified from the source-derived geometry. If this gets revisited,
 * any fix touching cluster index 1 needs a much narrower, more carefully
 * bounded x/y window than 2026-08-09's attempt used, verified against the
 * ramp curve specifically before shipping -- that curve is easy to catch
 * unintentionally since it shares the same cluster and a nearby x-range
 * as the rail segment over this person's head.
 */
